//? Title: Boyer–Moore: Exact Byte Matching and the Shifts It Actually Takes
//? Subtitle: Native bad-character and strong good-suffix tables, a DNA trace and a repeated-match worst case
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Boyer–Moore compares a pattern against the text from right to left, then skips
forward using bad-character and strong good-suffix rules. We first inspect a
five-byte example, retain the original DNA search and its six alignments, and
read the preprocessing tables, mismatch positions and comparison counts directly
from the canonical C++ search loop. A final experiment contrasts large skips,
unit shifts and repeated full matches. Skipping can reduce inspected bytes on
suitable inputs; this all-occurrences implementation can still do quadratic
comparison work when many long matches overlap.
)

\toc

> Simple example: shift two positions, then match

Search for ABC in XXABC. The first comparison is pattern[2]=C against
text[2]=A. A's rightmost pattern index is zero, giving bad-character shift
2-0=2. The good-suffix rule offers one, and the core takes max(1,2,1)=2.
The next alignment matches ABC at offset two, comparing C, B and A in that
order. The two visited alignments require four byte comparisons in total.

Both panels use actual records from `boyer_moore_trace`. Coloured cells show
compared bytes: red for the failed comparison and green for equal bytes.
Uncoloured cells in a visited alignment were not compared during that alignment.

```{cpp, file="figures/boyer_moore_small.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::string text="XXABC",pattern="ABC";
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::boyer_moore_trace(text,pattern);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(70,1100);
  for (std::size_t panel=0;panel<trace.alignments.size();++panel) {
    const auto& step=trace.alignments[panel];const double shift=9*panel;
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle cell;cell.width=.8;cell.height=.8;cell.padding=5;
    diagram.text({shift+2,2.2},std::format("alignment s = {}; {} comparison{}",
        step.position,step.comparisons,step.comparisons==1?"":"s"));
    for (std::size_t i=0;i<text.size();++i) {
      cell.fill={239,246,255};
      const bool compared=i>=step.position+pattern.size()-step.comparisons && i<step.position+pattern.size();
      if (compared) cell.fill=!step.matched&&i==step.position+step.mismatch_index?dm::RGB{254,226,226}:dm::RGB{220,252,231};
      diagram.node(std::format("t{}-{}",panel,i),{shift+i,0},std::string(1,text[i]),cell)
          .text({shift+i,1},std::format("{}",i),14);
    }
    for (std::size_t i=0;i<pattern.size();++i) {
      cell.fill={239,246,255};
      if (i>=pattern.size()-step.comparisons)
        cell.fill=!step.matched&&i==step.mismatch_index?dm::RGB{254,226,226}:dm::RGB{220,252,231};
      diagram.node(std::format("p{}-{}",panel,i),{shift+step.position+i,-1.5},std::string(1,pattern[i]),cell);
    }
    diagram.text({shift+2,-3.1},step.matched?std::format("full match at {}; next shift {}",step.position,step.shift):
        std::format("bad = {}, good = {}; take shift {}",step.bad_character_shift,step.good_suffix_shift,step.shift));
  }
  diagram.save_svg("build/boyer_moore_small.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/boyer_moore_small.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Text indices are byte offsets. The next shift after a full match is also recorded, even when it leaves the range of possible alignments.)
\alttext(ABC first aligns under XXA and mismatches C with A at text offset two. A two-position shift aligns it under ABC, producing a full match after three right-to-left comparisons.)

> Exact problem and the two safe shift rules

For text length n and pattern length m, return every start s with
0<=s<=n-m whose m bytes equal the pattern. Matches may overlap. The library
returns no matches for an empty pattern or one longer than the text. Strings
are byte sequences: embedded NUL and high bytes work, comparison is
case-sensitive, and UTF-8 offsets count bytes rather than characters.

At alignment s, suppose the first mismatch encountered from the right is at
pattern index j. The suffix pattern[j+1..m-1] already matched. If c is the
mismatching text byte, the raw bad-character proposal is j-last[c], where
last[c] is the rightmost pattern index containing c, or -1 if absent. It can
be negative when the rightmost occurrence lies to the right of j. The core
floors progress at one and combines it with the positive good-suffix shift:

$$
 d=\max\{1,\ j-\operatorname{last}[c],\ \operatorname{shift}[j+1]\}.
$$
\alttext(The next shift is the maximum of one, the raw bad-character shift and the strong good-suffix shift.)

The strong good-suffix rule looks for an earlier copy of the matched suffix
preceded by a different byte from pattern[j]. If none works, it aligns a
pattern prefix with a suffix of the matched part. The condition on the
preceding byte rules out an alignment known to repeat the same mismatch.
The core builds the shift array in two passes over border positions: first
record eligible repeated suffixes, then fill remaining entries using prefix
borders. Index zero is the full-match shift, based on the longest proper
border; it preserves overlapping occurrences.

Each rule excludes occurrences before its proposed next alignment. Taking
the larger safe lower bound remains safe. It does not prove that this choice
is fastest in elapsed time or that all bytes between alignment starts were
unread: each alignment can inspect several trailing bytes.

> Original DNA example: tables and actual search decisions

The original text GTTATAGCTGATCGCGGCGTAGCGGCGAA has 29 bytes, and the
pattern GCGGCG has six. There are 24 possible starting positions. The
following program prints both native tables and all visited alignments,
then plots each departure shift. The ordinary search and trace call the
same kernel; no report-side search or preprocessing implementation remains.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/boyer_moore_shifts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::string text="GTTATAGCTGATCGCGGCGTAGCGGCGAA",pattern="GCGGCG";
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::boyer_moore_trace(text,pattern);
  if (trace.matches!=dm::algorithms::boyer_moore_search(text,pattern)) return 1;
  std::print("| byte | rightmost pattern index (-1 = absent) |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (char byte:std::string("ACGT")) std::print("| {} | {} |\n",byte,trace.tables.last[static_cast<unsigned char>(byte)]);
  std::print("\n| suffix-start k | good-suffix shift[k] |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t k=0;k<trace.tables.good_suffix.size();++k)
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n",k,trace.tables.good_suffix[k]);
  std::print("\n| start s | result | equal suffix bytes | comparisons | raw bad | good | chosen shift |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> starts,shifts,match_starts,match_shifts;
  for (const auto& step:trace.alignments) {
    const auto result=step.matched?std::string("full match"):
        std::format("mismatch j={}, text {}",step.mismatch_index,char(step.text_byte));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",step.position,result,step.matched_suffix_length,
        step.comparisons,step.bad_character_shift,step.good_suffix_shift,step.shift);
    starts.push_back(step.position);shifts.push_back(step.shift);
    if (step.matched) {match_starts.push_back(step.position);match_shifts.push_back(step.shift);}
  }
  std::print("\nMatches: {}. Visited alignments: {} of {} possible starts. Search byte comparisons: {}.\n",
      trace.matches,trace.alignments.size(),text.size()-pattern.size()+1,trace.comparisons);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().bars(starts,shifts,"departure shift",dm::RGB{37,99,235},.7)
      .points(match_starts,match_shifts,"full match",dm::RGB{220,38,38},5)
      .title("GCGGCG: six visited alignments and their actual shifts")
      .x_label("pattern start offset s, bytes").y_label("departure shift, bytes")
      .y_limits(0,9).size(900,480).save_svg("build/boyer_moore_shifts.svg");
}
```
