Matches: [13, 21]. Visited alignments: 6 of 24 possible starts. Search byte comparisons: 17.
\image(figures/boyer_moore_shifts.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The two absent bytes A and T cause six-byte shifts at starts zero and six. Full matches occur at 13 and 21; the trace counts 17 search comparisons.)
\alttext(Six bars at starts zero, six, twelve, thirteen, sixteen and twenty-one have heights six, six, one, three, five and three. Red markers identify the full matches at thirteen and twenty-one.)

At s=16, the last G matches and the next comparison fails on absent A at
pattern index four. The raw bad-character shift is five, while good-suffix
shift[5] is two, so the chosen shift is five. At s=12, C is present in the
pattern and the chosen shift is one. Each full match shifts three, preserving
the pattern's GCG border. These are observations from this input, rather than
an average-case claim about all DNA sequences. A small alphabet can make
substrings recur frequently and limit skipping.

> Large skips, unit shifts and the all-match worst case

Fix text A repeated n=2048 times and vary pattern length m. Three deterministic
families separate the reasons for a low or high comparison count:

- Pattern B repeated m times: every mismatch is against a byte absent from the
  pattern, so each alignment costs one comparison and shifts m.
- Pattern A repeated m-1 times followed by B: the rightmost comparison fails
  immediately, but the mismatching A occurs at pattern index m-2. Shifts are
  one, so every possible start is visited with one comparison.
- Pattern A repeated m times: every start is a full overlapping match, the
  period shift is one, and the core rechecks all m bytes each time.

The last family makes exactly m(n-m+1) comparisons. With m proportional to
n, this is quadratic, even though the method can skip well on other inputs.
The implementation does not remember already matched regions across alignments.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/boyer_moore_comparisons.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::size_t n=2048;const std::string text(n,'A');
  std::vector<double> length,absent,unit,repeated;
  std::print("| m | B^m comparisons | A^(m-1)B comparisons | A^m comparisons | A^m matches |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t m:{4,8,16,32,64,128}) {
    const auto a=dm::algorithms::boyer_moore_trace(text,std::string(m,'B'));
    const auto u=dm::algorithms::boyer_moore_trace(text,std::string(m-1,'A')+'B');
    const auto r=dm::algorithms::boyer_moore_trace(text,std::string(m,'A'));
    if (a.comparisons!=n/m || u.comparisons!=n-m+1 || r.comparisons!=m*(n-m+1)) return 1;
    length.push_back(m);absent.push_back(double(a.comparisons)/n);
    unit.push_back(double(u.comparisons)/n);repeated.push_back(double(r.comparisons)/n);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",m,a.comparisons,u.comparisons,r.comparisons,r.matches.size());
  }
  auto matches=dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(length,repeated,"overlapping A^m",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .points(length,repeated,"",dm::RGB{220,38,38},4)
      .title("Overlapping full matches")
      .x_label("pattern length m, bytes").y_label("comparisons / text byte")
      .y_limits(0,160).size(700,460);
  auto misses=dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(length,unit,"unit-shift miss",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .lines(length,absent,"absent-byte skip",dm::RGB{22,163,74},2)
      .title("Two kinds of mismatch")
      .x_label("pattern length m, bytes").y_label("comparisons / text byte")
      .y_limits(0,1.4).size(700,460);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).size(1400,500)
      .title("Repeated full matches recheck m bytes per alignment")
      .add(matches).add(misses).save("build/boyer_moore_comparisons.svg");
}
```
