| case | text bytes | pattern bytes | match starts | visited alignments | comparisons |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: |
| overlapping matches | 4 | 2 | [0, 1, 2] | 3 | 6 |
| empty pattern | 3 | 0 | [] | 0 | 0 |
| pattern longer than text | 2 | 3 | [] | 0 | 0 |
| case-sensitive | 6 | 3 | [3] | 2 | 4 |
| embedded NUL | 5 | 2 | [1, 3] | 3 | 5 |
| high bytes | 3 | 1 | [0, 2] | 3 | 3 |
| negative raw bad shift | 4 | 4 | [] | 1 | 2 |

Negative-shift control: raw bad -1, good 4, chosen 4.
// result_end
\caption(NUL and high-byte data are described by case labels instead of writing nonprinting bytes into the report. Every returned position counts bytes.)

Preprocessing takes O(m+256) work and storage. Ordinary search allocates the
match offsets but no alignment records, and its worst-case comparison bound
for this implementation is O((n-m+1)m). A trace retains O(A+K) records for A
visited alignments and K matches, plus the tables. Both APIs borrow inputs
without mutation and own their outputs. Trace always preprocesses the pattern,
even for impossible searches; ordinary search returns immediately for empty
or oversized patterns. Signed border-index range and recorded counter overflow
are checked. Generated byte strings suffice for the algorithmic experiments;
no observational dataset is required.

> References

- R. S. Boyer and J Strother Moore (1977), A fast string searching algorithm,
  Communications of the ACM 20(10), 762–772.
  [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.1145/359842.359859).
- J Strother Moore, [the authors' algorithm page](https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~moore/best-ideas/string-searching/),
  including the qualification about recurring substrings on small alphabets.
